installed on the machine. The wiki's picture of the window shows the new row. Comments name CU Unlock, before CU
Tools.

Changelog: 24-11-2026 05:04:45

Breaking. The app is now called BC-250 Core Unlock, before BC-250 CPU Tools. The window leads through five steps
instead of opening a terminal: Scan, Test the stock cores, Unlock and restart, Test the new cores, and Install. It
runs bc250-cores-bisect.sh and bc250-cores-unlock.sh itself. Each step's row says the one thing to do now, with its
pill on that row, and a green bar says when you're finished. After the unlock and between attempts the window
restarts the board itself after a countdown, and it opens again after the login with the next step. A desktop
notification says when a step ends. A test blocks sleep while it runs. After a full power off in the middle of the
test, step 4 turns the new cores on again with bc250-cores-unlock.sh --apply. Install needs no restart, because the
cores are on already. The window's Unattended and Auto resume switches are gone, and so is the separate unlock part
with its verdict row. The script's --auto still works from a terminal. Uninstall also removes the window's autostart
entry.

Changelog: 24-11-2026 04:34:43

Added. bc250-cores-bisect.sh has two options for the coming step window. --scan reads the presence mask, the cores and
what a run needs, then exits without writing to the SMU. --only ITEM runs only the attempts of one item. The help
page lists them.

Changelog: 24-11-2026 04:29:56

Fixed. Every attempt ended as LOAD-FAIL with "temp-path '.' must be readable and writeable" when the test started from
the window. The window starts in its root-owned app folder, and stress-ng 0.22 refuses to start in a folder it can't
write, even for its CPU stressors. bc250-cores-bisect.sh now gives stress-ng the logs folder of its results as its
temp folder, so the script works from any folder.

