the script disables it and leaves the file. The wiki said the script carries on without rasdaemon when ras-mc-ctl is
missing, but the script stops then, and only warns and carries on when the service is not running or ras-mc-ctl fails.
The script's --help said that machine-check events are attributed to the tested CPUs, but the script counts every
machine-check line since the attempt started. The --help, the script's message for a run as root and the wiki said sudo
is used only for setpci and the kernel log, but the script also uses it to stop and start the governor's service, for
ras-mc-ctl and for a restart. After a cold boot before the new cores came up, the script said "Re-applying...", but it
offers the unlock again first. The words lspci, pciutils, util-linux, coreutils, taskset and ras-mc-ctl are added to
tools/text_check/words.txt. Nothing the app does has changed. Tests changed for the new wording: test_gate.py ("The
control failed. It runs on the stock 6C/12T", "isn't finished. 4 of 8"), test_help_page.py (the --auto line of the
options) and test_main_window.py ("restarts included", "Install failed (exit code 1). The log says why."). The wiki's note on a custom BIOS says it is another route that is not part of the suite, without a link (owner,
21-11-2026). The example unit bc250-cores-bisect-auto.service.example had a home folder with a user name in its
ExecStart; it is a placeholder under %h now.

